Extract the promises I made to the contact from a 1:1 conversation.

Author labels in the text:
- [me, <date>] — my message
- [<contact_name>, <date>] — the contact's message

A promise means only lines labeled [me, ...]: when I personally commit to doing something
specific for the contact.

DO NOT extract:
- the contact's promises and plans
- questions, past facts, recommendations
- plans without a clear commitment from me
- media without text ([voice/attachment])

Rules for summary:
- Specific action in first person: "send slides", "schedule a call to discuss details"
- Include the object of the action in summary
- Do not include "me" or the contact's name in summary

Minimum summary informativeness (CRITICAL):
- summary MUST include a concrete object of the action. Forbidden are one-word or generic formulations without an object: "look", "do", "add", "check", "fix", "investigate", "finish", "clarify", "reply", "write", "send" — without specifying WHAT exactly is to be looked at/done/sent.
- If the conversation does not reveal WHAT the promise targets (no object, topic, or result) — DO NOT extract the task. It is better to skip the promise than to create a useless task that no one will understand five minutes after creation without the original conversation.
- If the object of the action is ambiguous or vague ("I'll take a look", "I'll do it", "I'll reply later"), and the conversation does not clarify — DO NOT extract.
- Minimum for a valid summary: verb + concrete object. Acceptable examples: "send Q2 presentation slides", "call the contractor to discuss contract terms". Unacceptable examples (DO NOT extract): "Look", "Do", "Reply", "Send the file", "Write a message".

Rules for context:
- Start with "Promised to: <contact_name>" — use the name from the conversation header
- Add a brief context (1 sentence): the occasion for the promise
- Example: "Promised to: Vasily. In response to a request to send project materials."
- If context is obvious from summary — "Promised to: <name>." is sufficient

Rules for topic:
- Thematic area: component, system, meeting, or work domain
- Leave empty string if unclear

Deadline calculation:
- Use the "Current date and time" value from the user prompt as the reference point.
- If you can unambiguously calculate absolute time — return in ISO 8601 format with timezone.
- If unsure of the exact time — acceptable to return a natural English expression:
  "tomorrow", "tomorrow at 10", "on Friday", "by the weekend", "in 2 days", "by end of week".
- If no deadline is specified in the text at all — null.

Return a JSON array without additional text. If there are no promises OR none of the found promises can be turned into an informative summary with a concrete object — return [].
[{"summary": "what to do", "context": "Promised to: <name>. Context.", "topic": "topic or empty string", "project": "", "deadline": "ISO 8601 or null", "confidence": 0.0-1.0}]

Always respond in English.
